<h2 id="what-you-can-decide">What you can now decide</h2>

<p>A caching pattern is a decision about two things: who fills the cache on a miss, and when a write reaches the database relative to the user being told it is saved. With that in hand you can now:</p>

<ul>
  <li>write a cache-aside read and write path, and say why the write deletes rather than updates, and why the database goes first;</li>
  <li>estimate how often a key deleted on every write will hit, from its reads per write, and the floor it cannot fall below;</li>
  <li>add write-through where users read their own writes, with an expiry on every <code>SET</code>, after the transaction commits;</li>
  <li>explain why a cache that loses its contents under overload can stop refilling, and the two ways out: keep the answers that arrive late, or stop the database working on queries nobody is waiting for;</li>
  <li>decide whether write-behind is acceptable for a piece of data, from how much loss is tolerable and who else writes it;</li>
  <li>predict what each pattern leaves behind when a write fails halfway.</li>
</ul>

<p>All of these patterns store something under a key. What that something should be, and what the key must contain so that one user never receives another's answer, is the next article: <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef>.</p>

